<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!-- The optional WOFF 2 package: the web font format every pipeline actually emits.

       Optional for the same reason CupriFace.Svg and CupriFace.Lottie are, and the engine's promise
       is the same — you pay for what you use. An app that ships TTF, or registers fonts through the
       host, references none of this and carries none of it.

       WHY IT IS NOT IN THE ENGINE. Two costs the engine should not impose on every consumer. The
       format needs a real decoder, not a decompression call: Brotli, plus a glyf/loca transform to
       run backwards (seven parallel streams and a 128-case coordinate encoding). And in a browser it
       needs a native Brotli decoder that the .NET managed wrapper does not expose there — see
       Brotli.cs, which explains why and what it costs.

       WHAT IT DOES NOT NEED. No new dependency, on any platform: Brotli off the browser is
       System.IO.Compression's BrotliStream from the BCL, and in a browser it is the runtime pack's
       own brotli archives reached under dotnet/runtime's own module name. So no NuGet package, no
       NativeFileReference, no native asset of ours, nothing to sign, and no third-party licence
       entering this repository. The decoder is written from the W3C Recommendation, which carries
       royalty-free patent commitments from Google (Brotli) and Monotype (WOFF 2).

       REACH. Desktop, Android and the Mono wasm host decode WOFF 2. The NativeAOT-LLVM host cannot:
       its compiler pack ships zlib and no brotli archive at all. That host reports the limitation by
       name instead of rendering a substitute face, which is the failure this package exists to end -
       a binding typo and a refused font look identical once a face silently changes. -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <!-- The coordinate decoder and the Brotli entry point both take pointers: the glyph streams are
         walked as spans, and the native call hands out fixed buffers. -->
    <AllowUnsafeBlocks>true</AllowUnsafeBlocks>
    <Description>WOFF 2 web font support for CupriFace — the format Google Fonts serves and every font pipeline emits. Decodes to SFNT at runtime, so a font can be loaded dynamically rather than converted at build time. Brotli comes from the BCL off the browser and from the runtime's own archives on the Mono wasm host; no new dependency and no native asset of its own. Not available on the NativeAOT-LLVM web host, which ships no brotli archive.</Description>
    <PackageTags>cupriface;font;woff;woff2;webfont;brotli;opentype;truetype</PackageTags>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="../CupriFace/CupriFace.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
